1
00:00:00,691 --> 00:00:01,470
All right, everybody.

2
00:00:03,510 --> 00:00:04,830
We'll get started again.

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:12,390
Welcome back. I hope you filled up on coffee and other things appropriately so.

4
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:23,010
We have two speakers to follow before we head off for lunch.

5
00:00:25,201 --> 00:00:31,140
One thing I wanted to let you folks know is that our troll, in fact, here has

6
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:35,610
a name. Does anybody know the name of the troll?

7
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:40,350
A couple of folks, mostly people I know and have worked with.

8
00:00:41,340 --> 00:00:44,070
So the name of the troll is Gimimir Standardsson.

9
00:00:46,590 --> 00:00:49,830
If you want the history behind that, you can find me sometime this week and I'll

10
00:00:49,890 --> 00:00:53,401
tell you the long and sordid story of Gimimir.

11
00:00:55,470 --> 00:00:59,130
All right, so we will get started. I'm going to introduce Dag Andersen, who is

12
00:01:00,840 --> 00:01:06,690
one of our hosts here and works with GBIF

13
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:09,301
Norway. Dag, are you ready?

14
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:11,910
We'll get started.

15
00:01:22,230 --> 00:01:28,051
So I'll be brief. I want to introduce Marit Hetta from Tromsø, which is

16
00:01:28,110 --> 00:01:32,880
going to guide us through traditional data standards.

17
00:01:32,970 --> 00:01:34,770
And she's part of the CARE

18
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,910
Task Group to develop this. So welcome on the stage, Marit Hetta, Assistant

19
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:43,110
Professor in Tromsø.

20
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:00,601
Okay. So buoris, everyone,

21
00:02:02,010 --> 00:02:03,540
and thank you for having me here today,

22
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:07,080
and thank you for that introduction.

23
00:02:08,310 --> 00:02:14,190
So I'm a Sámi living in Bodegå, northern Norway or the Norwegian side

24
00:02:14,220 --> 00:02:14,940
of Sámi.

25
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:20,340
I have a background in biology, and

26
00:02:23,430 --> 00:02:29,400
the heart of my work is the meeting between Sámi knowledge and scientific knowledge

27
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:30,030
systems.

28
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:35,760
And I also work in education and knowledge transmission,

29
00:02:37,050 --> 00:02:41,851
and particularly how Sámi knowledge can be strengthened and passed on to future

30
00:02:41,910 --> 00:02:42,690
generations.

31
00:02:44,130 --> 00:02:47,671
So all of these interests come together in the topic I want to talk about today,

32
00:02:48,330 --> 00:02:51,930
Sámi biodiversity data and indigenous data governance.

33
00:02:53,101 --> 00:02:55,200
So I'm going to take a simple

34
00:02:56,490 --> 00:03:02,130
example throughout the presentation, lichen data from Sámi reindeer grazing

35
00:03:02,190 --> 00:03:07,740
lands, and then explore what happens when biodiversity data are collected, shared,

36
00:03:07,921 --> 00:03:08,760
and reused.

37
00:03:13,291 --> 00:03:19,171
So 20 years back, when I was in the middle of my master's degree in biology, I

38
00:03:19,201 --> 00:03:20,490
had the opportunity to

39
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:23,220
work as a field assistant

40
00:03:24,570 --> 00:03:28,740
in a project with NINA, the Norwegian Institute for Nature Research.

41
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:32,970
I joined the NINA researchers doing fieldwork

42
00:03:34,620 --> 00:03:40,080
on permanent monitoring plots close to my home town.

43
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:47,730
The monitoring program was established in 1998 to follow changes in the winter

44
00:03:47,791 --> 00:03:49,860
grazing areas on the Finnmark Plateau,

45
00:03:51,240 --> 00:03:52,080
where we have

46
00:03:53,430 --> 00:03:54,720
Sámi reindeer husbandry.

47
00:03:57,031 --> 00:03:58,860
So there were two parts to the monitoring.

48
00:03:59,310 --> 00:04:04,890
One used satellite data to look at vegetation over larger areas, and the other,

49
00:04:05,041 --> 00:04:10,351
which NINA was responsible for, involved returning to permanent monitoring plots on

50
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:10,951
the ground.

51
00:04:12,930 --> 00:04:17,880
The plots were distributed across winter grazing areas, and the idea was to return

52
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:21,781
to them regularly and document how the vegetation was changing.

53
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:26,490
The distribution of the plots looked like on the map up there,

54
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:32,220
and we flew with helicopter between the maps, and it was about 10 kilometers

55
00:04:32,281 --> 00:04:33,510
between the plots.

56
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:44,010
So when I participated in 2005, we returned to plots that had already been surveyed

57
00:04:44,130 --> 00:04:45,390
in 1998.

58
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:51,930
So we registered the vegetation, different species, estimated vegetation cover,

59
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:54,960
measured thickness of the lichen, and more.

60
00:04:56,701 --> 00:05:00,660
This meant that the researchers could compare the same location over time.

61
00:05:02,190 --> 00:05:06,810
And overall, the study actually found an increase in lichen during this period.

62
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:13,980
Across the plots, average lichen cover increased about 18% in '98 to

63
00:05:14,310 --> 00:05:20,220
28% in 2005, and average lichen thickness increased from 22 to 28

64
00:05:20,281 --> 00:05:20,910
millimeters.

65
00:05:22,531 --> 00:05:26,520
But that doesn't mean that everything was improving everywhere.

66
00:05:27,451 --> 00:05:32,130
There were considerable differences between areas, and the report discussed several

67
00:05:32,190 --> 00:05:38,040
possible explanations, including grazing pressure, snow conditions, precipitation,

68
00:05:38,130 --> 00:05:39,750
and other climatic factors.

69
00:05:41,010 --> 00:05:45,781
So for the researchers then, these measurements were a way of monitoring ecological

70
00:05:45,870 --> 00:05:49,020
change and the condition of winter grazing areas.

71
00:05:50,070 --> 00:05:54,300
And when I was there as a biology student, that was also how I understood what we

72
00:05:54,330 --> 00:05:54,990
were doing.

73
00:05:56,910 --> 00:06:02,550
But today, 20 years later, I look back at exactly the same fieldwork and the same

74
00:06:02,610 --> 00:06:07,680
lichen data, and I see another layer of questions, because these were not just data

75
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:12,300
about lichen. They were data collected from Sámi reindeer grazing lands.

76
00:06:13,201 --> 00:06:16,740
And that changes the question we might ask

77
00:06:18,210 --> 00:06:19,170
about those data,

78
00:06:20,520 --> 00:06:24,841
what they represent, how they're used, and who they matter to.

79
00:06:29,430 --> 00:06:32,790
So a biodiversity data point can have consequences.

80
00:06:34,201 --> 00:06:39,780
So here we have the dataset that was published with the report, a part of it,

81
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:44,790
so in this case, different species of lichen.

82
00:06:46,140 --> 00:06:51,870
So it looks quite simple at first sight, a species, a location, a date,

83
00:06:51,960 --> 00:06:53,010
perhaps a measurement,

84
00:06:55,831 --> 00:06:58,591
and a simple occurrence record.

85
00:07:00,390 --> 00:07:04,800
But the same record can look very different depending on who is looking at it.

86
00:07:06,841 --> 00:07:10,710
For a biodiversity infrastructure, it is an occurrence.

87
00:07:11,610 --> 00:07:14,430
For a researcher, it may be ecological evidence.

88
00:07:15,270 --> 00:07:19,680
For a planning authority, it could potentially become evidence used in decisions

89
00:07:19,770 --> 00:07:24,600
about land use. And for a reindeer herder, it may be information about pasture.

90
00:07:25,710 --> 00:07:30,390
And this is where the apparently simple lichen observation starts to become much

91
00:07:30,420 --> 00:07:31,290
more complicated.

92
00:07:32,581 --> 00:07:36,780
Reindeer move between seasonal grazing areas, and lichen is a particularly

93
00:07:36,841 --> 00:07:42,570
important winter food resource. So information about where lichen

94
00:07:42,660 --> 00:07:48,270
occurs, its condition, and how it changes over time can also be information about

95
00:07:48,331 --> 00:07:53,760
reindeer grazing lands. And those grazing lands are connected to Sámi livelihoods,

96
00:07:53,910 --> 00:07:57,390
rights, knowledge, and relationships with the land.

97
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:04,020
So information about lichen can become relevant far beyond biodiversity research.

98
00:08:04,651 --> 00:08:06,510
It can enter management decisions,

99
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:13,890
land use, planning, discussions about competing land uses, and

100
00:08:13,951 --> 00:08:16,050
disputes concerning grazing lands.

101
00:08:18,451 --> 00:08:23,520
It is still a record about lichen, but it exists within a much larger web of

102
00:08:23,581 --> 00:08:24,451
relationships.

103
00:08:25,710 --> 00:08:30,690
So the question is: Where does biodiversity data end and indigenous data begin?

104
00:08:32,461 --> 00:08:36,961
And those boundaries are not easy to draw.

105
00:08:38,370 --> 00:08:41,970
And it's also where indigenous governance starts to enter the picture.

106
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:47,490
But before we can talk about indigenous data sovereignty or indigenous data

107
00:08:47,550 --> 00:08:52,950
governance, we need to start somewhere more fundamental, with indigenous peoples'

108
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:54,901
rights and self-determination.

109
00:09:00,151 --> 00:09:04,950
So I don't want to give a legal lecture on UNDRIP, but it is an important starting

110
00:09:05,010 --> 00:09:08,940
point for understanding why we are talking about indigenous data sovereignty in the

111
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:09,690
first place.

112
00:09:10,944 --> 00:09:15,114
The United Nations declarations on the rights of indigenous peoples provides an

113
00:09:15,144 --> 00:09:18,234
international framework for the rights of indigenous peoples.

114
00:09:18,955 --> 00:09:24,024
UNDRIP was adopted by the UN General Assembly in 2007, following more than two

115
00:09:24,144 --> 00:09:27,864
decades of negotiations between indigenous peoples and states.

116
00:09:29,394 --> 00:09:33,924
It is a most comprehensive international instrument addressing the rights of

117
00:09:33,984 --> 00:09:38,575
indigenous peoples and established minimum standards for wellbeing and rights.

118
00:09:40,344 --> 00:09:46,014
These include rights relating to self-determination, participation in

119
00:09:46,075 --> 00:09:49,284
decision-making, lands and resources,

120
00:09:50,545 --> 00:09:55,795
culture, language, knowledge, and the ability of indigenous peoples to pursue own

121
00:09:55,854 --> 00:09:56,634
priorities.

122
00:10:01,825 --> 00:10:05,964
So there are several articles in UNDRIP that are particularly relevant when we

123
00:10:05,994 --> 00:10:09,114
start thinking about knowledge, data, and biodiversity.

124
00:10:10,404 --> 00:10:16,314
Articles 11 to 13 include the right of indigenous peoples to maintain, protect,

125
00:10:16,464 --> 00:10:21,744
practice, and develop cultural standards, traditions, and importantly,

126
00:10:22,404 --> 00:10:26,784
the right to transmit histories, languages, oral traditions, and knowledge to

127
00:10:26,844 --> 00:10:27,955
future generations.

128
00:10:29,484 --> 00:10:32,874
They also address questions of control and return.

129
00:10:33,774 --> 00:10:37,944
For example, Article 11 addresses redress, which may include

130
00:10:39,504 --> 00:10:44,904
restitution where cultural, intellectual, religious, or spiritual property has been

131
00:10:44,964 --> 00:10:47,934
taken without free, prior, and informed consent.

132
00:10:49,075 --> 00:10:54,864
And Article 12 explicitly addresses access and repatriation in relation to

133
00:10:54,955 --> 00:10:57,325
ceremonial objects and human remains.

134
00:10:59,604 --> 00:11:05,274
So these articles were not written specifically about biodiversity databases, but

135
00:11:05,304 --> 00:11:08,934
they give us an important foundation for the questions we're discussing today.

136
00:11:10,014 --> 00:11:14,904
Because when we talk about Sámi knowledge of biodiversity, landscapes, grazing

137
00:11:14,934 --> 00:11:19,224
conditions, we are also talking about knowledge that is connected to culture,

138
00:11:19,314 --> 00:11:23,904
language, land, and relationships, and knowledge that needs to be transmitted to

139
00:11:23,994 --> 00:11:25,194
future generations.

140
00:11:27,714 --> 00:11:32,844
So despite the growing recognitions of indigenous rights through UNDRIP and other

141
00:11:32,904 --> 00:11:37,644
international instruments, much of the research data concerning Sámi people is

142
00:11:37,674 --> 00:11:43,614
still held and governed by universities, state archives, museums, and research

143
00:11:43,674 --> 00:11:44,575
infrastructures,

144
00:11:45,774 --> 00:11:51,325
and these institutions may have legal, administrative, or custodial responsibility

145
00:11:51,384 --> 00:11:52,254
for the data.

146
00:11:54,414 --> 00:11:58,554
But this can mean the Sámi communities themselves have limited influence over

147
00:11:58,584 --> 00:12:04,524
decisions about use and stewardship, even when those data concern Sámi collective

148
00:12:04,584 --> 00:12:05,064
histories,

149
00:12:06,444 --> 00:12:08,094
culture, knowledge, and more.

150
00:12:11,124 --> 00:12:14,964
So the question is not simply whether we can preserve or make data accessible.

151
00:12:15,264 --> 00:12:18,684
It is also whether indigenous peoples can continue to maintain

152
00:12:20,124 --> 00:12:25,733
and transmit knowledge and have a meaningful role in decisions concerning material

153
00:12:25,825 --> 00:12:27,984
and information connected to that knowledge.

154
00:12:31,374 --> 00:12:34,434
So indigenous data sovereignty doesn't begin with data.

155
00:12:34,764 --> 00:12:38,634
It begins with self-determination and indigenous people's rights.

156
00:12:46,374 --> 00:12:51,114
So here I want to distinguish between two closely related concepts, indigenous data

157
00:12:51,204 --> 00:12:53,545
sovereignty and indigenous data governance.

158
00:12:55,224 --> 00:12:58,945
So indigenous data sovereignty is fundamentally about self-determination, rights,

159
00:12:59,004 --> 00:13:04,374
and authority. It recognizes the right of indigenous peoples to have authority over

160
00:13:04,434 --> 00:13:08,034
data that concerns our people, communities, land, and knowledge.

161
00:13:09,024 --> 00:13:12,654
Indigenous data governance is about putting that authority into practice.

162
00:13:14,064 --> 00:13:19,045
It concerns the policies, protocols, institutions, and decision-making processes

163
00:13:19,075 --> 00:13:24,204
that shape how data are collected, stored, accessed, interpreted, shared, and

164
00:13:24,265 --> 00:13:24,864
reused.

165
00:13:26,844 --> 00:13:31,914
But indigenous data governance is not simply another form of good data management.

166
00:13:33,414 --> 00:13:37,644
It requires us to think more fundamentally about whose knowledge counts, who holds

167
00:13:37,704 --> 00:13:41,575
authority, and how power is exercised within our data systems.

168
00:13:43,164 --> 00:13:47,274
And ultimately, the purpose is to support indigenous self-determination and

169
00:13:47,364 --> 00:13:49,195
long-term cultural continuity.

170
00:13:52,585 --> 00:13:54,894
So the two dimensions are in practice,

171
00:13:57,445 --> 00:14:01,434
and it's particularly useful when we talk about biodiversity data.

172
00:14:03,534 --> 00:14:05,424
So the first is data for governance.

173
00:14:05,874 --> 00:14:10,075
Indigenous peoples need access to data in order to make our own decisions and

174
00:14:10,134 --> 00:14:11,964
govern according to our own priorities.

175
00:14:13,164 --> 00:14:18,714
For Sámi communities, that include biodiversity data on reindeer grazing

176
00:14:18,774 --> 00:14:22,524
conditions and changes in ecosystems and so on.

177
00:14:23,634 --> 00:14:25,374
And the second is governance of data.

178
00:14:25,644 --> 00:14:27,954
This is about having authority over that data.

179
00:14:30,894 --> 00:14:34,734
So we can have access to data without having authority over how those data are

180
00:14:34,795 --> 00:14:39,265
governed. And equally, if we want meaningful indigenous governance, we also need

181
00:14:39,384 --> 00:14:43,045
access to the information necessary for our own decision-making.

182
00:14:48,474 --> 00:14:53,484
So for biodiversity data, this becomes particularly interesting because we might

183
00:14:53,545 --> 00:14:55,524
think, but these aren't data about people.

184
00:14:56,244 --> 00:14:59,724
These are data about species, ecosystems, plants, and animals.

185
00:15:01,104 --> 00:15:04,045
So then we move on to what are indigenous data?

186
00:15:08,424 --> 00:15:14,184
So indigenous data, we might think it's, for example, health data, demographic

187
00:15:14,274 --> 00:15:19,164
data, administrative data, or research involving indigenous participants.

188
00:15:19,825 --> 00:15:24,714
But it's much more than that. Indigenous data can include data, information, and

189
00:15:24,744 --> 00:15:29,964
knowledges in any format that impact indigenous peoples, nations,

190
00:15:30,024 --> 00:15:33,204
communities at both collective and individual levels.

191
00:15:34,825 --> 00:15:37,644
And this framework identifies three broad areas.

192
00:15:38,214 --> 00:15:43,343
There are data about us as individuals, things like health, administrative, legal,

193
00:15:43,374 --> 00:15:48,114
and social data. There are data about us as collectives, including languages,

194
00:15:48,204 --> 00:15:52,795
traditional and cultural information, knowledge systems, and ancestral and

195
00:15:52,854 --> 00:15:53,784
collective knowledge.

196
00:15:56,124 --> 00:15:58,825
And then it's data about our non-human relations.

197
00:15:59,515 --> 00:16:05,184
So this includes data about land, water, territories, ecosystems, plants, and

198
00:16:05,244 --> 00:16:08,244
animals. So suddenly, we're back to the

199
00:16:09,594 --> 00:16:13,104
lichen data, the lichen I showed you earlier.

200
00:16:14,664 --> 00:16:17,575
So the boundaries are not necessarily that simple.

201
00:16:19,674 --> 00:16:22,734
So the lichen occurs within Sámi reindeer grazing lands.

202
00:16:22,884 --> 00:16:26,515
They're connected to reindeer, to culture, to language,

203
00:16:29,784 --> 00:16:31,554
to grazing conditions, and so on.

204
00:16:32,814 --> 00:16:37,045
So biodiversity data do not necessarily have to contain information about Sámi

205
00:16:37,134 --> 00:16:39,984
person to have relevance for indigenous data governance.

206
00:16:43,134 --> 00:16:47,814
So I think this one is the most important point for the biodiversity

207
00:16:47,874 --> 00:16:48,835
infrastructures.

208
00:16:52,374 --> 00:16:58,104
So before we move further, I want to talk about the Sámi

209
00:16:58,164 --> 00:16:58,914
context.

210
00:17:02,575 --> 00:17:06,684
So there can be skepticism and caution toward research towards sharing data,

211
00:17:08,214 --> 00:17:10,314
and this does not come from nowhere.

212
00:17:14,785 --> 00:17:18,924
For generations, Sámi people have been researched, measured, classified, and

213
00:17:18,984 --> 00:17:22,404
documented by institutions outside Sámi society.

214
00:17:23,454 --> 00:17:28,765
This history includes colonization and assimilation and also racial biology,

215
00:17:29,334 --> 00:17:34,254
where Sámi people were measured and categorized as objects of scientific research.

216
00:17:35,394 --> 00:17:40,015
Data, photographs, human remains, cultural objects, and knowledge have been

217
00:17:40,044 --> 00:17:45,114
collected and taken into universities, museums, archives, and other institutions.

218
00:17:45,984 --> 00:17:50,694
And historically, Sámi people have often had very limited influence over what was

219
00:17:50,725 --> 00:17:56,664
collected, how it was interpreted, where it was kept, and who could access it, and

220
00:17:56,725 --> 00:17:58,734
how it could later be reused.

221
00:18:00,624 --> 00:18:05,214
The quotation on this slide illustrates how that history can continue to shape

222
00:18:05,274 --> 00:18:09,475
people's willingness to participate in research and to provide information.

223
00:18:10,884 --> 00:18:16,104
And this is not unique to Sámi. Similar questions about research relationships,

224
00:18:16,554 --> 00:18:17,364
extraction,

225
00:18:18,624 --> 00:18:22,494
and trust are discussed by indigenous peoples in many parts of the world.

226
00:18:24,564 --> 00:18:28,854
So one of the important tasks for research today is building and maintaining trust.

227
00:18:29,604 --> 00:18:33,294
But trust cannot simply be requested from Sámi communities.

228
00:18:33,864 --> 00:18:36,804
It has to be earned through the way research is done.

229
00:18:37,975 --> 00:18:43,765
And that means meaningful participation, transparency, relationships,

230
00:18:43,884 --> 00:18:48,265
accountability, and having a say in what happens to data after they have been

231
00:18:48,324 --> 00:18:48,954
collected.

232
00:18:52,884 --> 00:18:57,534
So back to the lichen data. At the beginning, I presented this as a quite

233
00:18:57,744 --> 00:19:00,475
straightforward biodiversity monitoring project.

234
00:19:01,314 --> 00:19:05,725
We had permanent plots, we identified species, we measured lichen cover and

235
00:19:05,784 --> 00:19:08,874
condition, and we compared changes over time.

236
00:19:10,015 --> 00:19:13,914
So we have a biodiversity record and observation of lichen.

237
00:19:15,954 --> 00:19:19,644
Put many of these observations together and we can say something about the

238
00:19:19,704 --> 00:19:22,884
distribution and condition of lichen across the landscape.

239
00:19:24,024 --> 00:19:27,774
But this particular landscape is also reindeer grazing land, and lichen is an

240
00:19:27,834 --> 00:19:30,174
important winter grazing resource for reindeer.

241
00:19:31,194 --> 00:19:34,374
So this data can also tell us something about grazing conditions,

242
00:19:35,694 --> 00:19:39,654
and this is where the data can begin to move beyond ecological monitoring.

243
00:19:40,644 --> 00:19:44,515
The data can be analyzed, combined with other information, and used in research and

244
00:19:44,574 --> 00:19:50,094
management. They may contribute to how environmental change and grazing conditions

245
00:19:50,184 --> 00:19:54,475
are understood and potentially to decisions concerning these landscapes.

246
00:19:55,674 --> 00:19:58,765
And those landscapes are not simply ecological spaces.

247
00:19:59,544 --> 00:20:01,734
They are connected to Sámi reindeer husbandry.

248
00:20:08,725 --> 00:20:12,834
So before I go further with this example, I want to make one thing very clear.

249
00:20:13,344 --> 00:20:18,654
I'm not using this case to criticize NINA or the researchers who carried out this

250
00:20:18,714 --> 00:20:24,234
work. This was valuable ecological monitoring, and I was fortunate to participate

251
00:20:24,294 --> 00:20:29,934
in it. I'm also looking back at research that was conducted almost 20 years ago

252
00:20:29,964 --> 00:20:33,924
through questions and frameworks that have developed considerably since then.

253
00:20:36,324 --> 00:20:38,904
So I'm using this as a thought experiment.

254
00:20:39,234 --> 00:20:43,884
If we looked at this data today through the lens of indigenous data governance,

255
00:20:44,334 --> 00:20:47,454
what additional questions might we ask?

256
00:20:48,954 --> 00:20:52,524
And here I want to look at what happens when this data move beyond the individual

257
00:20:52,584 --> 00:20:57,984
measurements. The monitoring data were analyzed and published in the NINA report.

258
00:21:00,054 --> 00:21:04,464
The report found that overall lichen cover had increased and that average lichen

259
00:21:04,524 --> 00:21:07,044
thickness had also increased.

260
00:21:08,304 --> 00:21:11,844
So from the ecological measurements, the researchers could document what they

261
00:21:11,904 --> 00:21:17,274
interpreted as a generally positive development in the lichen vegetation,

262
00:21:18,354 --> 00:21:21,444
although there were considerable differences between areas.

263
00:21:23,034 --> 00:21:26,394
And these findings also traveled outside the scientific report.

264
00:21:26,604 --> 00:21:31,254
Here, for example, the headline says, "Better grazing on Finnmarksvidda," which is

265
00:21:31,464 --> 00:21:33,654
a plateau in Northern Norway.

266
00:21:35,274 --> 00:21:39,504
The text underneath says that research shows that lichen is growing in Finnmark.

267
00:21:45,114 --> 00:21:47,154
And I find this transition interesting.

268
00:21:47,604 --> 00:21:50,634
We started with measurements of lichen cover and thickness.

269
00:21:51,204 --> 00:21:56,094
The scientific report interprets changes in the lichen vegetation, and then in

270
00:21:56,154 --> 00:21:58,824
public communication, this becomes better grazing.

271
00:21:59,994 --> 00:22:02,184
But what does better grazing actually mean?

272
00:22:03,354 --> 00:22:08,034
Can we determine that from the amount and thickness of lichen data alone?

273
00:22:10,374 --> 00:22:14,334
For a reindeer herder, grazing conditions involve much more than how much lichen is

274
00:22:14,394 --> 00:22:20,094
present. They also involve snow and ice conditions, whether the reindeer can

275
00:22:20,184 --> 00:22:25,734
actually access the lichen, weather, landscape disturbance, movement between

276
00:22:25,794 --> 00:22:29,274
seasonal grazing areas, and knowledge on how reindeer use the land.

277
00:22:31,674 --> 00:22:34,314
So this is where I see an important knowledge gap.

278
00:22:35,064 --> 00:22:40,074
The report gives us valuable ecological knowledge, but in this particular account,

279
00:22:40,164 --> 00:22:44,604
there's much less visibility of the knowledge and perspectives of the Sámi reindeer

280
00:22:44,634 --> 00:22:46,914
herders who use these grazing lands.

281
00:22:48,894 --> 00:22:51,774
That doesn't make the ecological data less valuable.

282
00:22:52,074 --> 00:22:55,374
It means that the ecological data are only one part of the picture.

283
00:22:59,754 --> 00:23:02,874
So this brings us back to the indigenous data governance.

284
00:23:04,734 --> 00:23:10,614
It is also about whose knowledge is included in interpreting those data, who

285
00:23:10,674 --> 00:23:13,044
gets to define what that findings mean,

286
00:23:14,334 --> 00:23:18,804
and whose perspectives are visible when these findings are communicated and used.

287
00:23:20,154 --> 00:23:24,294
So more lichen does not by itself tell us what better grazing means.

288
00:23:27,804 --> 00:23:32,274
So that's why we need FAIR principles for indigenous data governance.

289
00:23:33,294 --> 00:23:38,784
So many of you are familiar with FAIR, so I don't want to go through all of

290
00:23:39,204 --> 00:23:44,814
these in detail. So FAIR stands for collective benefit, authority to

291
00:23:44,844 --> 00:23:47,424
control, responsibility, and ethics.

292
00:23:48,534 --> 00:23:53,964
While FAIR focuses primarily on the characteristics that enable data to be found,

293
00:23:54,265 --> 00:23:58,794
accessed, combined, and reused, CARE brings attention to people, rights,

294
00:23:58,944 --> 00:24:01,674
relationships, and purposes in data governance.

295
00:24:06,294 --> 00:24:10,614
Collective benefits ask whether the data ecosystems are designed in ways that

296
00:24:10,734 --> 00:24:13,194
actually create benefits for indigenous peoples.

297
00:24:14,244 --> 00:24:19,044
Authority to control is about recognizing indigenous peoples' rights and interests

298
00:24:19,104 --> 00:24:24,624
in data and the authority in decisions about how those data are governed.

299
00:24:26,094 --> 00:24:30,024
Responsibility reminds us that working with indigenous data also creates

300
00:24:30,084 --> 00:24:35,154
responsibilities to build relationships, strengthen capacity, and recognize

301
00:24:35,214 --> 00:24:37,884
indigenous languages, knowledge, and worldviews.

302
00:24:39,804 --> 00:24:44,544
And ethics ask us to consider indigenous rights and wellbeing throughout the entire

303
00:24:44,604 --> 00:24:48,384
data life cycle, including possible future uses of the data.

304
00:24:51,684 --> 00:24:55,314
So CARE gives us an important framework for thinking differently about data

305
00:24:55,344 --> 00:24:56,064
governance.

306
00:25:03,475 --> 00:25:06,084
So Carol and colleagues make an important distinction here.

307
00:25:06,414 --> 00:25:11,244
FAIR and CARE are complementary. They are not competing approaches.

308
00:25:12,504 --> 00:25:16,015
But they ask us to do somewhat different things.

309
00:25:17,064 --> 00:25:22,284
Assessing whether a data set is FAIR can largely be a technical exercise.

310
00:25:22,884 --> 00:25:28,164
We can ask whether the data are findable, accessible, interoperable, and reusable.

311
00:25:28,794 --> 00:25:31,794
CARE cannot be addressed only by looking at the data set.

312
00:25:32,334 --> 00:25:34,584
CARE requires engagement with people.

313
00:25:35,904 --> 00:25:40,314
And importantly, this engagement cannot simply happen at the end of a research

314
00:25:40,344 --> 00:25:43,944
project when we are preparing a data set for publication.

315
00:25:44,814 --> 00:25:48,894
CARE needs to be considered throughout the data life cycle, from the question we

316
00:25:49,014 --> 00:25:53,574
ask and the way data are collected, through governance and analysis, to sharing,

317
00:25:54,084 --> 00:25:55,554
reuse, and future use.

318
00:25:57,114 --> 00:26:01,524
So making data technically reusable is not the same thing as making their reuse

319
00:26:01,614 --> 00:26:03,114
appropriate or ethical.

320
00:26:10,734 --> 00:26:14,814
And that creates a practical question: How do we operationalize CARE?

321
00:26:15,954 --> 00:26:19,554
And this is also a question we have been working with in Sámi.

322
00:26:24,564 --> 00:26:28,194
So one important development was the first international conference on Sámi

323
00:26:28,254 --> 00:26:32,245
research data governance held in Tromsø in January 2023.

324
00:26:33,264 --> 00:26:37,824
The conference grew out of the Giida Sámi network and earlier online seminars that

325
00:26:37,855 --> 00:26:42,654
had brought together Sámi and non-Sámi researchers and people working with research

326
00:26:42,715 --> 00:26:44,574
data in Norway, Sweden, and Finland.

327
00:26:45,834 --> 00:26:50,605
The purpose was to create a space where experiences could be shared and begin

328
00:26:50,665 --> 00:26:54,954
discussing what indigenous data governance actually means in a Sámi and Nordic

329
00:26:55,014 --> 00:26:55,764
context.

330
00:26:57,415 --> 00:27:01,614
CARE was an important framework for the conference, but the question was really how

331
00:27:01,674 --> 00:27:05,904
principles like CARE could be understood and eventually put into practice in Sámi.

332
00:27:08,844 --> 00:27:12,774
Indigenous data governance is about indigenous authority and self-determination, so

333
00:27:12,804 --> 00:27:17,514
we also need Sámi-led spaces where researchers, institutions, organizations, and

334
00:27:17,605 --> 00:27:22,674
people working with data can discuss what appropriate governance should look like

335
00:27:22,764 --> 00:27:24,024
in our own context.

336
00:27:26,934 --> 00:27:30,355
And the responses to that first conference showed that there was considerable

337
00:27:30,415 --> 00:27:32,154
interest in doing exactly that.

338
00:27:37,687 --> 00:27:42,336
There was broad participation from Sámi organizations, academia, governmental

339
00:27:42,427 --> 00:27:47,946
organizations, and NGOs, including Sámi Parliament, the Sámi Council, and the

340
00:27:48,036 --> 00:27:50,616
Expert Ethical Committee for Sámi Health Research.

341
00:27:52,086 --> 00:27:54,516
And the range of topics was also quite broad.

342
00:27:55,056 --> 00:28:00,187
There were discussions on ethics, archives and repositories, health data,

343
00:28:00,937 --> 00:28:05,736
school research, machine learning, different contexts across Sámi, and practical

344
00:28:05,796 --> 00:28:07,866
approaches about data governance.

345
00:28:09,666 --> 00:28:13,836
Another important outcome was that several of the conference contributions were

346
00:28:13,866 --> 00:28:19,056
developed further and published in a thematic issue of Acta Borealia on Indigenous

347
00:28:19,116 --> 00:28:20,406
research data governance.

348
00:28:28,026 --> 00:28:32,466
So a working group was also established to strengthen the network and continue the

349
00:28:32,496 --> 00:28:33,246
collaboration.

350
00:28:35,496 --> 00:28:40,056
So the conference was an important meeting point, but it did not emerge in

351
00:28:40,146 --> 00:28:45,576
isolation. It grew out of an emerging network in Sámi and was also connected to a

352
00:28:45,606 --> 00:28:49,866
much broader international movement around Indigenous data sovereignty and

353
00:28:49,927 --> 00:28:51,336
Indigenous data governance.

354
00:28:54,906 --> 00:28:59,946
The Global Indigenous Data Alliance, or GIDA, was established in 2019.

355
00:29:00,366 --> 00:29:05,106
It is an international network for Indigenous researchers, data practitioners, and

356
00:29:05,166 --> 00:29:10,326
policy advocates working to advance Indigenous data sovereignty and Indigenous data

357
00:29:10,356 --> 00:29:10,986
governance.

358
00:29:12,366 --> 00:29:16,776
GIDA's work is about asserting Indigenous peoples' rights and interests in data,

359
00:29:17,286 --> 00:29:22,296
but importantly, it is also about data for self-determination, ensuring that

360
00:29:22,356 --> 00:29:26,916
Indigenous peoples have access to and can use data for their own priorities,

361
00:29:27,396 --> 00:29:28,957
wellbeing, and decision-making.

362
00:29:30,127 --> 00:29:33,516
So there are really two sides here, having authority over data concerning

363
00:29:33,576 --> 00:29:35,496
Indigenous peoples and having the data

364
00:29:36,846 --> 00:29:39,187
that Indigenous peoples need for their own governance.

365
00:29:41,856 --> 00:29:47,616
GIDA has connected networks and initiatives in different parts of the world.

366
00:29:47,796 --> 00:29:53,766
In Sámi, we have GIDA Sámi. It was established in 2021, and the

367
00:29:53,796 --> 00:29:57,216
reason for establishing GIDA Sámi was quite simple.

368
00:29:58,056 --> 00:30:02,946
Global principles need to be understood and operationalized in the local Indigenous

369
00:30:03,006 --> 00:30:03,636
context.

370
00:30:05,556 --> 00:30:09,036
So GIDA Sámi, therefore, has three main objectives.

371
00:30:10,957 --> 00:30:16,776
First, to promote, adapt, and operationalize the CARE principles in Sámi context.

372
00:30:18,306 --> 00:30:22,896
Second, to increase awareness of care within research, memory, and archival

373
00:30:22,957 --> 00:30:28,326
institutions. And third, to advance the discussion about Sámi data governance

374
00:30:28,386 --> 00:30:29,106
principles.

375
00:30:31,056 --> 00:30:35,466
Ultimately, the aim is to strengthen Sámi data governance, ensure that data can

376
00:30:35,526 --> 00:30:39,516
better serve the needs and priorities of contemporary Sámi society.

377
00:30:43,146 --> 00:30:45,486
So there are other things happening as well,

378
00:30:48,486 --> 00:30:50,616
and I just want to briefly mention these,

379
00:30:52,986 --> 00:30:57,696
because these are discussions that are moving from principles toward practice.

380
00:30:59,256 --> 00:31:04,896
So one recent development is the IEE 2890:2025 standard on the

381
00:31:04,927 --> 00:31:06,906
provenance of Indigenous peoples' data.

382
00:31:08,106 --> 00:31:12,756
It provides guidance for describing and recording the provenance of data relating

383
00:31:12,817 --> 00:31:16,836
to Indigenous peoples, including their cultures, lands, and knowledge systems.

384
00:31:18,516 --> 00:31:23,046
What is particularly relevant here is that provenance is connected not only to data

385
00:31:23,106 --> 00:31:27,996
integrity, but also to questions of Indigenous participation, control, and

386
00:31:28,056 --> 00:31:31,207
governance as data circulate and are reused.

387
00:31:32,646 --> 00:31:38,226
And the standard explicitly identifies biodiversity research as one of the areas

388
00:31:38,286 --> 00:31:39,666
where this is relevant.

389
00:31:42,187 --> 00:31:45,696
Another development is particularly

390
00:31:47,346 --> 00:31:50,076
relevant for this community. In 2025,

391
00:31:52,086 --> 00:31:57,457
GBIF established an Indigenous data governance task group looking at how care can

392
00:31:57,486 --> 00:31:59,677
be supported within the GBIF network.

393
00:32:01,356 --> 00:32:06,127
This includes work with local context labels and notices and exploring how

394
00:32:06,187 --> 00:32:09,726
Indigenous context can remain connected to biodiversity data.

395
00:32:11,466 --> 00:32:16,266
This work is still ongoing, so I don't want to present it as a finished solution.

396
00:32:17,526 --> 00:32:21,187
But I think it's significant that these questions are now being considered within

397
00:32:21,246 --> 00:32:24,096
biodiversity data infrastructures itself.

398
00:32:26,406 --> 00:32:27,666
So more things happening.

399
00:32:30,216 --> 00:32:35,796
These things are not limited to biodiversity infrastructures or to Sámi.

400
00:32:36,636 --> 00:32:40,116
Internationally, we're seeing many different attempts to put Indigenous data

401
00:32:40,177 --> 00:32:45,306
governance into practice. One example is the development of guidelines for the

402
00:32:45,336 --> 00:32:48,457
governance of Indigenous peoples' data in scholarly publishing.

403
00:32:51,636 --> 00:32:56,346
This reminds us that governance does not end when data collection or a research

404
00:32:56,377 --> 00:33:00,666
project ends. Data and knowledge continue to travel through publications,

405
00:33:01,026 --> 00:33:03,906
repositories, and other research systems.

406
00:33:06,546 --> 00:33:10,656
And then we're also seeing Indigenous peoples developing own infrastructure.

407
00:33:11,046 --> 00:33:15,846
And this example from Aotearoa, New Zealand, is a Maori-owned data storage network

408
00:33:16,296 --> 00:33:21,486
designed to enable Maori data to be stored within Maori-controlled infrastructure.

409
00:33:22,596 --> 00:33:25,476
So here, data sovereignty becomes very concrete.

410
00:33:26,016 --> 00:33:30,366
It is not only about principles, but also about ownership, infrastructures, and

411
00:33:30,427 --> 00:33:31,056
control.

412
00:33:32,736 --> 00:33:38,106
So things are happening in many levels, and there is no single technical solution.

413
00:33:42,696 --> 00:33:45,156
So then let's return back to the lichen.

414
00:33:49,446 --> 00:33:53,286
So I'm not looking back at this project to ask what the researchers did wrong.

415
00:33:53,556 --> 00:33:57,187
Instead, I want to use this data as a thought experiment.

416
00:33:58,356 --> 00:34:03,696
If we were collecting and sharing this lichen data today with what we know

417
00:34:04,716 --> 00:34:09,187
about Indigenous data governance, care, and Sámi research ethics, what additional

418
00:34:09,246 --> 00:34:14,766
questions might we ask? A biodiversity record can carry quite a lot of information.

419
00:34:15,756 --> 00:34:19,236
We can record the species, the location, the date, and the observer.

420
00:34:19,297 --> 00:34:22,536
We can add measurements, identifiers, and other metadata.

421
00:34:24,486 --> 00:34:28,146
And through biodiversity infrastructures, those data can become findable,

422
00:34:28,236 --> 00:34:31,866
accessible, and reusable far beyond the original research project.

423
00:34:33,366 --> 00:34:38,766
But what about Sámi context, knowledge provenance, authority, responsibilities for

424
00:34:38,856 --> 00:34:44,377
reuse, and so on? And how could we better align this data with care?

425
00:34:58,806 --> 00:34:58,927
So

426
00:35:00,636 --> 00:35:06,096
what does it actually require in practice to align this data with care?

427
00:35:07,866 --> 00:35:10,956
So before we do that, I want to mention these

428
00:35:11,016 --> 00:35:16,596
Sámi ethical guidelines for research.

429
00:35:17,766 --> 00:35:22,297
And this is an important development in Sámi that we need to include.

430
00:35:23,196 --> 00:35:28,536
In 2024, new ethical guidelines for research involving the Sámi people in Finland

431
00:35:28,567 --> 00:35:33,966
were published. These guidelines bring Sámi ethical considerations into the

432
00:35:33,996 --> 00:35:39,456
research process itself, including how research is planned, how relationships with

433
00:35:39,516 --> 00:35:44,496
Sámi communities are built, and how research that may affect Sámi people and

434
00:35:44,556 --> 00:35:46,746
communities is carried out.

435
00:35:48,966 --> 00:35:51,666
And in the same year, the Sámi Council adopted

436
00:35:54,666 --> 00:35:57,456
the Sámi Ownership and Data Access Principle, SODA.

437
00:35:58,626 --> 00:36:03,636
SODA builds on care but makes some things particularly explicit in a Sámi context.

438
00:36:04,146 --> 00:36:08,317
It speaks about collective benefits, Sámi authority to control, Sámi ownership,

439
00:36:08,406 --> 00:36:09,996
responsibility, and ethics.

440
00:36:11,436 --> 00:36:15,336
And importantly, these questions concern the whole data lifecycle, collecting,

441
00:36:15,427 --> 00:36:17,766
storage, use, sharing, and future reuse.

442
00:36:19,146 --> 00:36:23,136
So together, these developments remind us that Indigenous data governance is not

443
00:36:23,196 --> 00:36:26,766
something we simply add to a dataset at the point of publication.

444
00:36:27,996 --> 00:36:32,646
It is an iterative process that needs to be considered from the beginning of the

445
00:36:32,706 --> 00:36:38,526
research and revisited as the data are collected, interpreted, stored,

446
00:36:38,616 --> 00:36:39,606
shared, and reused.

447
00:36:43,272 --> 00:36:47,143
And so that also brings us back very clearly to the question of ownership and

448
00:36:47,172 --> 00:36:52,692
authority. Who has the right and decision-making power in relation to Sámi data?

449
00:36:57,462 --> 00:37:00,222
So here, the CARE data maturity model

450
00:37:01,632 --> 00:37:03,702
is one way to operationalize care.

451
00:37:06,612 --> 00:37:09,552
One thing I find particularly useful about this model

452
00:37:11,802 --> 00:37:17,472
is that it presents care as an iterative process rather

453
00:37:17,562 --> 00:37:18,312
something that

454
00:37:19,542 --> 00:37:21,762
we do once the project is ended.

455
00:37:24,762 --> 00:37:28,662
So in the beginning, we start by recognizing the context and establishing the

456
00:37:28,722 --> 00:37:31,032
principles and values that should guide the work.

457
00:37:33,102 --> 00:37:37,482
Then we assess where we are, identify priorities, document progress, and

458
00:37:37,542 --> 00:37:39,852
importantly, review, reflect, and improve.

459
00:37:44,262 --> 00:37:49,782
So my colleagues here to the right

460
00:37:52,032 --> 00:37:53,322
have also been working with

461
00:37:54,852 --> 00:37:58,182
what this could mean specifically in Sámi research context.

462
00:37:59,352 --> 00:38:04,932
This paper brings together the CARE principles and Sámi ethical guidance and

463
00:38:05,022 --> 00:38:07,692
translates them into more practical recommendations.

464
00:38:09,102 --> 00:38:14,082
For example, collective benefit means asking Sámi partners what they actually need

465
00:38:14,143 --> 00:38:17,682
from the research and considering benefits and possible harms.

466
00:38:18,882 --> 00:38:23,623
Authority to control means thinking about consent, collective interests, data

467
00:38:23,682 --> 00:38:28,992
governance plans, and how Sámi people are represented in decisions about access,

468
00:38:29,172 --> 00:38:30,132
use, and reuse.

469
00:38:31,452 --> 00:38:36,312
Responsibility includes relationships, cultural competence, and documenting

470
00:38:36,373 --> 00:38:38,623
governance in the data management process.

471
00:38:39,672 --> 00:38:45,042
And ethics include transparency, appropriate conditions for access and reuse, and

472
00:38:45,072 --> 00:38:45,942
benefit-sharing.

473
00:38:47,862 --> 00:38:52,212
The important point is that these decisions begin before the data are collected and

474
00:38:52,272 --> 00:38:54,643
continue throughout the life cycle of the data.

475
00:38:58,752 --> 00:39:00,792
So FAIR and CARE for the lichen data.

476
00:39:02,442 --> 00:39:07,482
On the left is the kind of biodiversity record we started with, a scientific name,

477
00:39:07,572 --> 00:39:10,452
a location, a data, and an observer.

478
00:39:11,623 --> 00:39:17,143
If we apply the questions from the CARE framework and the Sámi contextualization I

479
00:39:17,232 --> 00:39:21,432
showed on the previous slide, we begin to see additional questions around the

480
00:39:21,492 --> 00:39:22,002
record.

481
00:39:23,202 --> 00:39:26,202
For collective benefit, who needs this data?

482
00:39:26,742 --> 00:39:30,942
Could monitoring priorities also respond to the information needs within Sámi

483
00:39:30,972 --> 00:39:36,012
reindeer herding communities? For authority to control, are relevant Sámi rights

484
00:39:36,042 --> 00:39:40,123
holders involved in decisions about collecting, access, and sharing, and reuse?

485
00:39:42,342 --> 00:39:46,602
Responsibility asks what responsibilities come with collecting and stewarding this

486
00:39:46,632 --> 00:39:49,362
data and what relationships need to be maintained?

487
00:39:50,652 --> 00:39:53,172
And ethics asks us to consider how the data,

488
00:39:54,402 --> 00:39:58,362
their interpretation, and their future reuse could affect Sámi interests and

489
00:39:58,393 --> 00:40:02,202
whether Sámi knowledge and interpretations are appropriately represented.

490
00:40:04,602 --> 00:40:07,812
At the same time, we want biodiversity data to be FAIR.

491
00:40:08,712 --> 00:40:13,123
We want them to be findable. We want them to be accessible under appropriate

492
00:40:13,182 --> 00:40:18,072
conditions. We want systems to be able to understand and exchange them, and we want

493
00:40:18,102 --> 00:40:19,422
the data to be reusable.

494
00:40:20,952 --> 00:40:24,342
But when we put CARE and FAIR beside each other like this, another question

495
00:40:24,402 --> 00:40:29,772
appears. As we make the lichen data easier to discover, share, and reuse, how do

496
00:40:30,402 --> 00:40:34,962
we make sure that relevant context, authority, and responsibilities are not lost?

497
00:40:35,893 --> 00:40:37,873
FAIR can help this lichen data travel.

498
00:40:38,112 --> 00:40:41,862
CARE asks us to consider the governance around it.

499
00:40:51,012 --> 00:40:55,782
So that brings us to the biodiversity infrastructure itself, and these are things

500
00:40:55,812 --> 00:40:58,662
that the GBIF Indigenous Task Force is working with.

501
00:40:59,772 --> 00:41:03,702
So if you take this CARE question seriously, what could they mean when biodiversity

502
00:41:03,762 --> 00:41:06,732
data are shared through infrastructure such as GBIF?

503
00:41:08,472 --> 00:41:10,062
So there may be three stages.

504
00:41:11,982 --> 00:41:16,692
Data that are already there, the reuse of those data, and new data collections.

505
00:41:17,862 --> 00:41:22,962
First, there are already biodiversity data in GBIF that may be significant in Sámi

506
00:41:23,022 --> 00:41:26,143
context. Lichen is an obvious example.

507
00:41:27,402 --> 00:41:32,502
How do we recognize when apparently ordinary biodiversity data also have particular

508
00:41:32,562 --> 00:41:35,202
significance for Sámi communities and interests?

509
00:41:36,222 --> 00:41:41,143
And if that significance is identified, could mechanisms such as local context help

510
00:41:41,772 --> 00:41:44,172
make some of that context visible?

511
00:41:45,792 --> 00:41:48,972
The second question is what happens when those data are reused?

512
00:41:49,932 --> 00:41:54,282
Once biodiversity data are aggregated, downloaded, combined with other datasets,

513
00:41:54,402 --> 00:41:59,682
and used for new purposes, they can travel a very long way from the context in

514
00:41:59,712 --> 00:42:01,393
which they were originally collected.

515
00:42:02,262 --> 00:42:05,172
So can relevant context remain connected to the data?

516
00:42:06,672 --> 00:42:08,922
Can provenance and reuse be traced?

517
00:42:10,212 --> 00:42:15,432
And can information about governance and responsibilities continue to accompany the

518
00:42:15,492 --> 00:42:17,472
data as they move between systems?

519
00:42:20,172 --> 00:42:22,632
The third situation is new data collection.

520
00:42:23,592 --> 00:42:29,262
Rather than trying to add care at the end, care can be considered when the research

521
00:42:29,322 --> 00:42:34,542
is designed. Who should participate? What governance arrangements are appropriate?

522
00:42:35,082 --> 00:42:37,782
What should be included in the data management plan?

523
00:42:38,202 --> 00:42:41,472
And what protocols might be needed for future access and reuse?

524
00:42:43,602 --> 00:42:48,552
So for existing data, we may need to recognize and reconnect context.

525
00:42:49,302 --> 00:42:53,742
For reused data, we need to ask whether that context and governance can travel with

526
00:42:53,772 --> 00:42:59,412
the data. And for new data, we have the opportunity to build

527
00:42:59,502 --> 00:43:01,662
care into the process from the beginning.

528
00:43:04,032 --> 00:43:07,092
So I want to emphasize that I'm presenting these as

529
00:43:07,151 --> 00:43:12,012
questions rather than technical solutions.

530
00:43:15,342 --> 00:43:17,712
So I want to finish where I started with the lichen.

531
00:43:21,373 --> 00:43:26,202
Almost 20 years ago, when I was measuring lichen on the Finnmark Plateau, I

532
00:43:26,262 --> 00:43:30,192
understood what we were collecting primarily as ecological data.

533
00:43:30,792 --> 00:43:34,452
A species, a location, a measurement, a change over time.

534
00:43:35,322 --> 00:43:40,242
But looking at those same data today through a lens of indigenous data governance,

535
00:43:40,393 --> 00:43:41,952
I see something more.

536
00:43:43,212 --> 00:43:48,042
The lichen record is still a biodiversity record, but it does not exist in

537
00:43:48,132 --> 00:43:53,802
isolation. The lichen is part of an ecosystem, but it is also connected to reindeer

538
00:43:53,862 --> 00:43:58,512
grazing, and through that, to the Sámi reindeer husbandry, land use, knowledge,

539
00:43:58,572 --> 00:44:01,362
livelihoods, and relationships with the land.

540
00:44:01,932 --> 00:44:07,182
So ecological data can have significance and consequences far beyond the individual

541
00:44:07,272 --> 00:44:08,292
species record.

542
00:44:10,332 --> 00:44:14,292
And this connects to the broader understanding of indigenous data that I have

543
00:44:14,352 --> 00:44:19,452
talked about today. Indigenous data are not only data about indigenous people.

544
00:44:19,692 --> 00:44:25,002
They can also include data about our lands, waters, ecosystems, plants, animals,

545
00:44:25,092 --> 00:44:26,832
and other non-human relations.

546
00:44:28,932 --> 00:44:33,462
And we are seeing important developments in Sápmi through care, Sámi ethical

547
00:44:33,522 --> 00:44:38,262
guidelines, Soada, Giida Sápmi, and wider discussions about research data

548
00:44:38,292 --> 00:44:42,672
governance. So we are moving from broad principle towards thinking much more

549
00:44:42,732 --> 00:44:45,222
concretely about governance in practice.

550
00:44:47,052 --> 00:44:51,822
Because making biodiversity data open, fair, and reusable can create enormous

551
00:44:51,882 --> 00:44:57,822
value. But as those data become easier to discover, aggregate, and reuse,

552
00:44:58,002 --> 00:45:01,902
we also need to think about what may be lost when they move away from their

553
00:45:01,932 --> 00:45:03,042
original context.

554
00:45:05,022 --> 00:45:10,873
So biodiversity infrastructure can carry a species name, location, a

555
00:45:10,932 --> 00:45:15,552
date, and provenance. The challenge is how it can also support relevant context,

556
00:45:15,643 --> 00:45:18,012
relationships, rights, and responsibilities.

557
00:45:19,992 --> 00:45:22,602
So maybe this is the question I want to leave with you today.

558
00:45:23,022 --> 00:45:27,672
When biodiversity data travel, does their governance travel with them?

559
00:45:33,373 --> 00:45:33,612
Thank

560
00:45:33,672 --> 00:45:42,582
you.

