00:51:18,324 --> 00:51:22,285
Okay, thanks. I think it's going now. So I'm going to talk-- Okay.

627
00:51:22,344 --> 00:51:24,954
I'm going to talk to you. I'm going to talk fast, can't talk to the movie now.

628
00:51:25,344 --> 00:51:30,174
I'm going to talk about the transition from data to knowledge, and I'm going to

629
00:51:30,234 --> 00:51:32,844
start with a question. How do you know something?

630
00:51:33,775 --> 00:51:36,084
Of course, we learn a lot of knowledge.

631
00:51:36,324 --> 00:51:40,104
We get that passed down from our parents, from our professors, et cetera.

632
00:51:40,494 --> 00:51:43,434
But as scientists, we're really in the business of making new knowledge.

633
00:51:43,764 --> 00:51:49,405
We want answers to questions like, how do global impact or biodiversity loss

634
00:51:49,494 --> 00:51:52,164
impact the world? What is plant phenology?

635
00:51:52,464 --> 00:51:53,994
How do we know how

636
00:51:55,704 --> 00:51:59,815
the seasonal timing of plants, flowers, and reproductive and other vegetative

637
00:51:59,905 --> 00:52:03,504
structures are impacted by climate change or other factors?

638
00:52:04,584 --> 00:52:07,734
How does the fossil record help us understand human evolution?

639
00:52:11,454 --> 00:52:17,155
What mechanisms are responsible for the outbreak of pests or

640
00:52:17,184 --> 00:52:20,844
diseases, and how can we use that to understand human health, agriculture, other

641
00:52:20,905 --> 00:52:26,244
applied sciences? Why are some animals brightly colored and aposematic,

642
00:52:27,144 --> 00:52:31,584
and why do others remain sort of dull and hidden within the background?

643
00:52:35,815 --> 00:52:40,254
Animal behavior. What can studying the behavior of animals help teach us about the

644
00:52:40,285 --> 00:52:44,275
behavior of organisms in general, whether it's about population, individual

645
00:52:44,334 --> 00:52:48,324
interactions, mating behavior, defense, et cetera?

646
00:52:49,254 --> 00:52:54,984
We might also want to answer questions about species interactions, whether they're

647
00:52:55,074 --> 00:52:57,565
mutually beneficial, herbivorous,

648
00:53:00,504 --> 00:53:05,844
a crab using the shell of a snail. Are they parasitic

649
00:53:05,905 --> 00:53:06,655
interactions?

650
00:53:07,974 --> 00:53:13,434
We can also use information data to understand decomposition and its impacts on

651
00:53:13,494 --> 00:53:14,304
ecosystems.

652
00:53:16,464 --> 00:53:22,285
What about other behavior aspects

653
00:53:22,464 --> 00:53:28,315
such as, or evolution aspects as mimicry, herbivory? What do animals eat?

654
00:53:29,664 --> 00:53:34,224
Why do cacti do these crazy things sometimes? I'd love to know the answer to that.

655
00:53:34,285 --> 00:53:36,594
Look, there's a fruit growing out the side of a stem.

656
00:53:37,315 --> 00:53:41,424
What the heck is this thing, even? I don't know. I assume it's a beetle larvae.

657
00:53:41,754 --> 00:53:44,184
Why is this banded gecko trying to get into my house?

658
00:53:46,644 --> 00:53:51,414
And why do reindeer's eyes change colors during winter season, become more blue?

659
00:53:51,775 --> 00:53:57,204
For the record, the answer is because the continued dilation of their pupils causes

660
00:53:57,264 --> 00:54:00,894
a layer at the back of their eyes to change its reflectivity, making them more

661
00:54:00,954 --> 00:54:06,625
sensitive to blue light, which presumably helps them see the contrast of

662
00:54:06,655 --> 00:54:10,315
lichen against the snow in the wintertime, finding the food that they need.

663
00:54:13,014 --> 00:54:18,804
Let's see if this works now. Yep. So science is really about answering these

664
00:54:18,864 --> 00:54:23,065
questions. How do we do science? We go from observation, the collection of data,

665
00:54:23,574 --> 00:54:25,494
generation of evidence, to knowledge.

666
00:54:25,884 --> 00:54:30,384
A couple of great examples using biodiversity, they use this method to address

667
00:54:30,444 --> 00:54:36,294
scientific questions are the work of Barbara McClintock, who used maize

668
00:54:36,594 --> 00:54:41,035
and genetics to discover transposons and eventually won a Nobel Prize for her work

669
00:54:41,094 --> 00:54:45,894
there. Another contemporary example is Gila monsters, animals that live in my

670
00:54:45,924 --> 00:54:51,655
backyard. Work on their metabolism, help people understand GLP-1 inhibitors, which

671
00:54:51,684 --> 00:54:55,254
are now widely used in medicines such as Ozempic for weight loss,

672
00:54:55,974 --> 00:55:00,714
alcoholism control, and many other uses.

673
00:55:03,756 --> 00:55:08,376
So just a talk, what I'm going to talk about today, again, I'm going to focus on

674
00:55:08,946 --> 00:55:11,886
how do we transform data into knowledge. I'm going to cover six topics.

675
00:55:12,456 --> 00:55:16,386
First of all, what are data? We all say data, but I want to talk a little bit about

676
00:55:16,446 --> 00:55:19,536
how I'm defining it within the context of what I'm talking about from an

677
00:55:19,626 --> 00:55:25,116
ontological perspective. What do we do to transform that data into knowledge, and

678
00:55:26,677 --> 00:55:28,476
through the use of evidence generation there?

679
00:55:28,956 --> 00:55:31,837
Now we're going to throw AI into the mix because it really is transforming our

680
00:55:31,896 --> 00:55:34,027
world. How do we take that into consideration?

681
00:55:34,686 --> 00:55:40,057
The real importance of ontologies, standards, and data governance, and then what

682
00:55:40,116 --> 00:55:44,226
TABB's role and how important it is for groups like TABB to make this happen

683
00:55:44,286 --> 00:55:47,706
effectively. So you're probably noticing here, there's some really consistent

684
00:55:47,856 --> 00:55:49,866
themes with the two talks earlier today.

685
00:55:50,256 --> 00:55:55,027
Happy to say that I haven't seen radical disagreement about the ideas that I had,

686
00:55:55,236 --> 00:55:56,976
which were presented by the other speakers.

687
00:55:57,036 --> 00:56:00,277
But I hope that I can bring a somewhat different perspective to it.

688
00:56:01,536 --> 00:56:04,626
So let me start with talking about what are data. What do I think data are?

689
00:56:05,106 --> 00:56:08,587
So data are the result of an observation or measurement.

690
00:56:10,386 --> 00:56:15,366
They are written down, recorded. They can be text, they can be numbers.

691
00:56:15,786 --> 00:56:18,396
They can be a video or an audio recording.

692
00:56:20,136 --> 00:56:22,626
They're almost always a sample of something.

693
00:56:22,896 --> 00:56:27,427
So it's true that if you take a herbarium specimen of an individual, you have the

694
00:56:27,486 --> 00:56:29,706
whole represent-- Well, not in plants.

695
00:56:29,736 --> 00:56:31,146
You usually just have a little piece of the plant.

696
00:56:31,506 --> 00:56:33,696
But for an insect, you may have the entire insect there.

697
00:56:34,057 --> 00:56:38,436
But that plant, if it's a leaf, that leaf is a representation of the whole plant,

698
00:56:38,527 --> 00:56:43,236
was a representation of the population, which is then a

699
00:56:43,297 --> 00:56:46,686
representation of the species, et cetera. Right?

700
00:56:47,286 --> 00:56:51,846
So they're data, but like most data, most measurements, say you measure my blood

701
00:56:51,876 --> 00:56:55,656
pressure, you might measure it four times, but it's not a complete record of my

702
00:56:55,686 --> 00:56:59,677
blood pressure. So it is a sample of the thing that you're trying to understand.

703
00:57:00,846 --> 00:57:05,436
And data, really key distinction. Data are intended to represent the truth.

704
00:57:05,976 --> 00:57:08,856
They may be a sample, but from them, we want to know,

705
00:57:10,476 --> 00:57:14,047
is something present or absent? What's its size? What condition it is in?

706
00:57:14,677 --> 00:57:18,096
What's its identity or species? What relationships are there to other things?

707
00:57:18,186 --> 00:57:21,307
So data are not just random recordings of information.

708
00:57:21,337 --> 00:57:25,716
They are intended to represent the truth, and so that's a difference between data

709
00:57:25,777 --> 00:57:27,996
and any piece of information that you might have out there.

710
00:57:29,886 --> 00:57:35,616
From an ontological perspective, a widely used ontology within the biosciences is

711
00:57:35,677 --> 00:57:37,626
the Information Artifact Ontology.

712
00:57:38,047 --> 00:57:43,027
And so IAO defines data as a data entity, which is

713
00:57:44,677 --> 00:57:47,916
an information content entity that is intended to be one or more truthful

714
00:57:48,006 --> 00:57:52,686
statements about something, modulo measure imprecision or other systematic errors,

715
00:57:53,346 --> 00:57:59,316
and is constructed, acquired, and acquired by a method which reliably tends

716
00:57:59,346 --> 00:58:01,536
to produce approximately truthful statements.

717
00:58:02,226 --> 00:58:06,846
So you can see that my perspective about what data is, it aligns very closely with

718
00:58:06,876 --> 00:58:09,456
the Information Artifact Ontology.

719
00:58:10,206 --> 00:58:15,815
And so in that ontology, data entity is a subclass of information

720
00:58:15,876 --> 00:58:20,406
content entity, which is defined as a generically independent continuant that is

721
00:58:20,496 --> 00:58:25,566
about something. I won't go into the definition of a generically independent

722
00:58:25,626 --> 00:58:26,346
continuant.

723
00:58:28,297 --> 00:58:33,906
But you can see that information is largely larger, a category of things

724
00:58:33,966 --> 00:58:37,686
that are described, and data entity is a very specific

725
00:58:39,006 --> 00:58:41,166
subtype of an information content entity.

726
00:58:44,106 --> 00:58:45,246
So then what is knowledge?

727
00:58:46,476 --> 00:58:46,716
And

728
00:58:48,666 --> 00:58:52,686
I'm going to be talking about knowledge in a very sort of traditional, scientific,

729
00:58:53,047 --> 00:58:57,156
Western understanding of what knowledge is and scientific knowledge.

730
00:58:57,726 --> 00:59:02,226
As we've seen already today, there are different types of knowledge and different

731
00:59:02,256 --> 00:59:07,236
ways of defining what the knowledge is that you know or that's relevant to your

732
00:59:07,356 --> 00:59:11,616
situation. But knowledge and the study of knowledge has been around for many, many

733
00:59:11,646 --> 00:59:16,266
years. Most cultures throughout history have a goddess or a god of knowledge or

734
00:59:16,326 --> 00:59:19,956
wisdom. So that's Athena's symbol from ancient Greece.

735
00:59:20,316 --> 00:59:26,166
This is Saraswati. I'm sorry, now I can't remember the name exactly

736
00:59:26,527 --> 00:59:29,106
, but this is an ancient Hindu goddess of knowledge and wisdom.

737
00:59:29,496 --> 00:59:34,297
So why is knowledge so important? Because it's survival, right?

738
00:59:34,416 --> 00:59:37,206
Imagine if you were born and thrown into the wilderness and you had to figure out

739
00:59:37,266 --> 00:59:40,986
everything for yourself. Probably wouldn't make it very long, aside from the

740
00:59:41,047 --> 00:59:44,016
biological needs of not being able to eat or talk or walk.

741
00:59:46,716 --> 00:59:49,057
This is probably a pyramid that many of you have seen before.

742
00:59:49,116 --> 00:59:52,356
It's the knowledge pyramid, and what's clear here is that data is at the very

743
00:59:52,416 --> 00:59:55,866
bottom. It's the foundation of all knowledge.

744
00:59:56,916 --> 01:00:02,047
In this case, this structure, information is derived from data.

745
01:00:03,366 --> 01:00:06,396
Notice that's the opposite of what I showed you in the Information Artifact

746
01:00:06,486 --> 01:00:12,366
Ontology, where a data entity is a type of information content

747
01:00:12,427 --> 01:00:15,516
entity. That's because information is defined differently than here.

748
01:00:15,636 --> 01:00:18,156
So here's a really important role of ontologies.

749
01:00:18,216 --> 01:00:23,856
How you define something is going to define how you understand that entity within

750
01:00:23,916 --> 01:00:26,736
the worldview that you're trying to work within.

751
01:00:28,266 --> 01:00:33,366
Wisdom, a little bit beyond our talk here, but without knowledge, you can't have

752
01:00:33,396 --> 01:00:33,786
wisdom.

753
01:00:36,036 --> 01:00:41,076
I hope everyone here is working towards wisdom and putting your data, your

754
01:00:41,106 --> 01:00:44,616
information, and your knowledge to good use to behave wisely and make the world a

755
01:00:44,646 --> 01:00:45,307
better place.

756
01:00:47,166 --> 01:00:47,376
So

757
01:00:48,606 --> 01:00:53,646
if we want to transform data into knowledge, this is a fairly--

758
01:00:55,177 --> 01:00:58,057
It's not the only way you can get there, but this is the way that we generally

759
01:00:58,177 --> 01:01:01,896
operate within science in the world that most of us are living in.

760
01:01:02,436 --> 01:01:06,036
So first, you have to have meaning of the data. You need to know what it means.

761
01:01:06,786 --> 01:01:10,416
You have to establish trust. This is about provenance, context.

762
01:01:11,136 --> 01:01:13,626
You have to define the relevance of the data.

763
01:01:13,746 --> 01:01:14,196
So

764
01:01:15,966 --> 01:01:20,047
are the data that you're working with relevant to the context in which you're

765
01:01:20,106 --> 01:01:25,686
working? And now that I work in a biomedical regulatory world for

766
01:01:25,716 --> 01:01:28,896
regulators, this phrase, context of use, comes up constantly.

767
01:01:28,986 --> 01:01:33,185
So it's not just what data do you have, but how relevant are they to the question

768
01:01:33,216 --> 01:01:35,646
that you're trying to answer? It's very specific.

769
01:01:37,297 --> 01:01:42,636
Finally, evidence. So you combine the meaning of the data, your trust for it, do

770
01:01:42,696 --> 01:01:47,047
you know who collected it, what it means, with the relevance to the application and

771
01:01:47,076 --> 01:01:50,016
the question that you're trying to answer, and then you create evidence.

772
01:01:50,196 --> 01:01:53,886
And that evidence is what you use to create knowledge.

773
01:01:54,846 --> 01:01:58,927
So here's a couple examples of evidence that you might find through careful

774
01:01:59,016 --> 01:02:02,376
observation in the natural world. You got a feather.

775
01:02:03,486 --> 01:02:07,807
There's evidence right there that a bird of that particular species is present.

776
01:02:08,226 --> 01:02:13,177
If you can recognize the feather, you can say, "Okay, I know bird X was here." I

777
01:02:13,206 --> 01:02:16,986
don't know what this was from. Possibly a blue jay.

778
01:02:18,636 --> 01:02:23,706
You can now probably look at it and extract some DNA evidence for it and gain even

779
01:02:23,736 --> 01:02:27,666
more information. Looking at the picture on the right, you've got a paw print.

780
01:02:27,756 --> 01:02:30,006
I believe that's a raccoon. Not 100% sure.

781
01:02:30,726 --> 01:02:35,226
You've got strong evidence that a raccoon was there if you recognize that print.

782
01:02:35,916 --> 01:02:38,226
So which of these is stronger evidence?

783
01:02:40,536 --> 01:02:44,376
I would say the feather is stronger because you can identify exactly what-- You got

784
01:02:44,436 --> 01:02:49,506
a physical specimen here. Unless you're a real expert and the paw prints of the

785
01:02:49,566 --> 01:02:53,376
animal that you're working from, that you're looking for, are really unique to that

786
01:02:53,466 --> 01:02:57,427
animal, someone could have come along with a little mold and stamped them in the

787
01:02:57,486 --> 01:03:01,027
ground, right? So how much trust do you have in the paw prints versus how much

788
01:03:01,116 --> 01:03:05,797
trust do you have in the feather? So different kinds of data, different levels of

789
01:03:05,856 --> 01:03:09,307
trust, different strengths of evidence there.

790
01:03:15,277 --> 01:03:15,516
So

791
01:03:17,886 --> 01:03:23,166
I've talked already about how getting from data to knowledge requires evidence,

792
01:03:23,886 --> 01:03:26,496
but there are people that know things without any evidence.

793
01:03:28,116 --> 01:03:32,496
And I'm not talking about the kind of indigenous knowledge that was discussed in

794
01:03:32,527 --> 01:03:36,576
the last talk because, again, there are different ways, different types of evidence

795
01:03:36,786 --> 01:03:39,277
being passed down for different reasons that you can know.

796
01:03:40,356 --> 01:03:43,146
But there are many cases in the world where people know things

797
01:03:44,797 --> 01:03:47,496
with very little evidence or misleading evidence.

798
01:03:48,846 --> 01:03:51,246
We're living this a lot in my country right now.

799
01:03:52,476 --> 01:03:56,976
So here's a classic example of knowledge without strong evidence.

800
01:03:57,066 --> 01:04:02,797
So a lot of people know or claim that there is an unknown species-like

801
01:04:02,856 --> 01:04:05,826
organism living in the Himalayas, known as a Yeti.

802
01:04:06,307 --> 01:04:10,356
Some people also think species like this may live in northern Norway.

803
01:04:11,623 --> 01:04:13,603
And there's pseudo-evidence, right?

804
01:04:13,842 --> 01:04:17,652
So there are stories, there have been sightings, there are footprints passed down,

805
01:04:18,522 --> 01:04:22,753
and there are even samples in collections, hair, bones, et cetera.

806
01:04:23,562 --> 01:04:25,512
And those are traceable. So

807
01:04:27,072 --> 01:04:31,512
what happened is someone took the evidence that did exist, the data that did exist,

808
01:04:31,572 --> 01:04:34,901
those specimens, and actually did an analysis against them.

809
01:04:35,322 --> 01:04:39,672
So they sequenced a number of them and were able to turn them into

810
01:04:41,112 --> 01:04:44,922
actual knowledge that is consistent with the scientific method that we're using

811
01:04:44,952 --> 01:04:48,762
here. So those data turned out to belong to bears and one dog.

812
01:04:49,152 --> 01:04:53,202
There was not actually a known primate species living in the Himalayas.

813
01:04:53,742 --> 01:04:54,012
So

814
01:04:55,542 --> 01:04:58,452
out of this, they got unexpected scientific value.

815
01:04:58,512 --> 01:05:03,822
So new information about the evolutionary history of these Himalayan bears that was

816
01:05:03,883 --> 01:05:09,042
not known before. It wasn't that the study just disproved that there are no Yetis

817
01:05:09,103 --> 01:05:12,012
living there, but they actually were able to understand something about the

818
01:05:12,492 --> 01:05:17,802
evolutionary history and diversity of Himalayan bears.

819
01:05:20,472 --> 01:05:22,633
So this is where we throw AI into the mix.

820
01:05:24,432 --> 01:05:28,362
I don't need to go into details because we had a really great talk this morning

821
01:05:28,422 --> 01:05:32,262
from Tanya about what AI can do, machine learning, all the tools that it can really

822
01:05:32,292 --> 01:05:37,152
help you. But let's think about this for a second. Does AI generate data?

823
01:05:38,562 --> 01:05:44,413
I posit that it does. AI can generate these new data entities specifically as

824
01:05:44,442 --> 01:05:46,811
defined by the information artifact ontology.

825
01:05:47,383 --> 01:05:52,962
It can create text, images, synthetic datasets, it can make predictions, it can

826
01:05:53,353 --> 01:05:57,072
classify. Again, got some great examples of that in the talk this morning, so I

827
01:05:57,072 --> 01:05:58,062
don't need to go into that.

828
01:05:59,623 --> 01:06:01,603
And it can infer values. So

829
01:06:03,522 --> 01:06:08,322
it's generating data. What it's not doing is generating new empirical observations

830
01:06:08,353 --> 01:06:10,512
of the world, at least not yet.

831
01:06:12,163 --> 01:06:17,892
So if a model, for example, predicts the trait distribution within a species,

832
01:06:19,753 --> 01:06:25,633
is that evidence? It's that the model is producing new

833
01:06:25,692 --> 01:06:31,152
data, but that is not the same thing as going out and measuring the distribution of

834
01:06:31,212 --> 01:06:32,652
a trait within an organism, right?

835
01:06:32,742 --> 01:06:36,913
So it's more of a hypothesis saying, "Here's generated data.

836
01:06:38,292 --> 01:06:43,362
Do I trust it? Is it real?" So that's the next question. Does AI create knowledge?

837
01:06:44,623 --> 01:06:46,962
So it can reproduce knowledge.

838
01:06:48,582 --> 01:06:52,902
AI is amazing at synthesizing information into

839
01:06:54,582 --> 01:06:56,982
insights, into information that we already know.

840
01:06:57,103 --> 01:07:00,883
Again, great example about the machine learning algorithms this morning, talking

841
01:07:00,942 --> 01:07:05,413
about beak size, for example, and shape. It can create candidate knowledge.

842
01:07:06,582 --> 01:07:12,192
So it can create new hypotheses, describe new relationships or explanations or

843
01:07:12,222 --> 01:07:16,812
predictions. Another awesome example this morning about how AI is generating these

844
01:07:16,873 --> 01:07:18,913
kinds of candidates of knowledge.

845
01:07:21,642 --> 01:07:25,902
It can help create new knowledge when those inferences become sufficiently

846
01:07:25,962 --> 01:07:29,172
supported by evidence. So this is, again, the role of evidence here.

847
01:07:29,592 --> 01:07:29,802
So

848
01:07:31,032 --> 01:07:33,222
do I trust what AI's discovered?

849
01:07:34,572 --> 01:07:37,722
It definitely can discover things that we don't know. It has already.

850
01:07:37,782 --> 01:07:43,572
It's doing it every day. But it only becomes knowledge if we have a reason to

851
01:07:43,603 --> 01:07:48,103
believe it's true. So is it really evidence? How do we know if it's evidence?

852
01:07:48,672 --> 01:07:53,772
Do we trust it? What is the context of use? What is the provenance of the data?

853
01:07:53,862 --> 01:07:56,623
How were they collected? What analyses were done on it?

854
01:07:57,163 --> 01:08:01,873
Whether you're using hand calculations on a piece of paper or using AI to do it,

855
01:08:01,932 --> 01:08:05,322
you still need to have those pieces of information to know whether or

856
01:08:07,392 --> 01:08:09,522
not you're willing to accept it as knowledge.

857
01:08:11,082 --> 01:08:11,292
So

858
01:08:13,692 --> 01:08:17,083
knowledge is one thing when we're going out, and this is still happening.

859
01:08:17,142 --> 01:08:22,032
Again, every day, many, many scientists are working where they have a hypothesis,

860
01:08:22,152 --> 01:08:27,133
they go out and collect some data, they translate, they test the hypothesis, they

861
01:08:27,312 --> 01:08:28,782
publish that as evidence for

862
01:08:30,343 --> 01:08:32,652
confirmation or rejection of their hypothesis.

863
01:08:33,192 --> 01:08:38,262
If you're the person collecting the data, you might think that writing a tag with a

864
01:08:38,353 --> 01:08:42,972
specimen ID, writing the number of the specimen on your little file, taking notes

865
01:08:43,062 --> 01:08:45,282
in your notebook, that that's sufficient.

866
01:08:45,312 --> 01:08:47,862
And it is sufficient for your study that you're trying to prove.

867
01:08:48,432 --> 01:08:53,142
But secondary data use, the only way that we're really able to scale the kind of

868
01:08:53,202 --> 01:08:58,062
inquiry that we need to keep up with the changes in the world, is through reuse of

869
01:08:58,123 --> 01:09:00,792
the data. So you're standing there in a collection

870
01:09:02,112 --> 01:09:04,212
with that puzzled look on your face.

871
01:09:05,172 --> 01:09:09,163
You can tell from this person, who some of us know, even though you can't see their

872
01:09:09,222 --> 01:09:13,392
face, you can see the, "What's going on here?" So you need more information.

873
01:09:13,452 --> 01:09:14,112
So this is where

874
01:09:16,093 --> 01:09:16,452
trust,

875
01:09:18,282 --> 01:09:24,042
standards, governance, and ontologies come in. How do we create that kind of trust?

876
01:09:25,333 --> 01:09:29,922
GBIF, standards organizations. So since I've switched from working in biodiversity

877
01:09:29,982 --> 01:09:35,262
primarily to working in biomedical science primarily, I have really grown to

878
01:09:35,442 --> 01:09:37,482
appreciate the importance of standards bodies.

879
01:09:38,322 --> 01:09:40,333
I didn't appreciate it when I worked within TDWG.

880
01:09:40,603 --> 01:09:43,242
I knew we were important, blah, blah, blah, but now that I'm there, now that we

881
01:09:43,302 --> 01:09:43,692
actually have

882
01:09:44,922 --> 01:09:49,992
regulations in the European Union and the United States, there are actually laws

883
01:09:50,052 --> 01:09:52,093
telling us what we can believe and what we can't.

884
01:09:53,593 --> 01:09:54,343
We don't trust,

885
01:09:55,782 --> 01:10:00,312
at least for whether or not the FDA is going to approve a medicine, they don't

886
01:10:00,402 --> 01:10:06,312
trust an academic-funded, NIH-funded study who's developed a great ontology

887
01:10:06,432 --> 01:10:11,112
to back up their claims. They want a standards organization that is international,

888
01:10:11,232 --> 01:10:15,133
independent, is going to be around 10, 20 years from now.

889
01:10:15,672 --> 01:10:21,192
So TDWG's made it since, when was that first? I think 1985.

890
01:10:22,182 --> 01:10:25,392
It's not bad, 40 years. I have a feeling that it's going to be here in another 40

891
01:10:25,422 --> 01:10:30,942
years. And so standards organizations like TDWG are really crucial

892
01:10:31,002 --> 01:10:34,452
here to creating this kind of knowledge, evidence, and trust.

893
01:10:35,982 --> 01:10:38,832
I'm not going to say a whole lot about standards here. They're what you do.

894
01:10:38,892 --> 01:10:42,252
You know this. They're the core of TDWG.

895
01:10:42,612 --> 01:10:46,512
They include data structures, models, vocabularies, exchange formats.

896
01:10:47,383 --> 01:10:50,353
Standards are what allow us to communicate and share data.

897
01:10:51,252 --> 01:10:55,392
Even if we speak different languages, when we're on the telephone, or writing a

898
01:10:55,422 --> 01:10:58,272
letter to one another, we can use standards to help there.

899
01:10:59,022 --> 01:11:02,802
Same thing with data governance. Governance controls

900
01:11:04,362 --> 01:11:06,552
who can access data, what they can do with it.

901
01:11:07,212 --> 01:11:10,782
We have a lot of modern systems that support complex governance.

902
01:11:11,742 --> 01:11:12,432
Great talk,

903
01:11:13,692 --> 01:11:14,682
the last talk about

904
01:11:16,122 --> 01:11:19,272
how do we let the governance travel with the information?

905
01:11:20,593 --> 01:11:24,402
Not going to give you a technical talk about that, but I would say, Rob and I were

906
01:11:24,432 --> 01:11:27,762
talking about that, like, "Can we actually do this?" And I said, "Yes." And I

907
01:11:27,792 --> 01:11:31,272
thought, "Well, no, but three to five years, we're going to be able to do this.

908
01:11:31,542 --> 01:11:36,312
We're going to have the structures in place to make governance portable along with

909
01:11:36,343 --> 01:11:42,103
the data." This is something that within CPATH, the work that my

910
01:11:42,133 --> 01:11:43,512
department does is

911
01:11:44,952 --> 01:11:50,412
all about managing governance. And CPATH has been around for 20 years now, and it

912
01:11:50,472 --> 01:11:55,152
is the governance, the very strong and very flexible governance that we use that

913
01:11:55,212 --> 01:11:59,112
gives us credibility and trust and lets people contribute their data, share their

914
01:11:59,172 --> 01:12:01,843
data through us that they would not share with anybody else.

915
01:12:02,593 --> 01:12:06,552
And so that's that last bit. Good governance makes altruism possible.

916
01:12:08,022 --> 01:12:12,162
One of the most common excuses that people or companies will use about why they

917
01:12:12,192 --> 01:12:16,812
can't share their data is, "Well, because I can't control who can see it." Well,

918
01:12:17,202 --> 01:12:19,603
now you can, and so they don't have an excuse anymore.

919
01:12:20,112 --> 01:12:23,622
And so they're willing to do it more often than what you might expect.

920
01:12:26,112 --> 01:12:29,442
Governance is great. Standards are great. Controlled vocabularies are great.

921
01:12:30,822 --> 01:12:36,672
And they can improve data interoperability quite a lot, but they only have loose

922
01:12:36,732 --> 01:12:40,633
semantics, if any. This is actually a slide I pulled out from a presentation from

923
01:12:42,103 --> 01:12:43,032
2017.

924
01:12:44,383 --> 01:12:46,212
It's still applicable. Why make a new slide?

925
01:12:46,242 --> 01:12:48,103
Because it's still very much the truth.

926
01:12:49,482 --> 01:12:52,362
So we need semantics, and this is where ontologies come in.

927
01:12:54,192 --> 01:12:59,353
Ontology is kind of two things, the philosophical study of being or the nature of

928
01:12:59,412 --> 01:13:01,812
existence, and the features of its entities.

929
01:13:01,872 --> 01:13:07,212
That's a bust of Socrates, who was one of the original ontologists back in ancient

930
01:13:07,242 --> 01:13:07,603
Greece.

931
01:13:10,002 --> 01:13:13,633
From a practical standpoint, an ontology is an artifact.

932
01:13:13,722 --> 01:13:17,022
It's a structured vocabulary that encodes the semantics of the entities in a

933
01:13:17,082 --> 01:13:22,122
domain. And just like FAIR principles, the key here is that it is both human and

934
01:13:22,152 --> 01:13:27,745
machine readable. Ontologies are encoded in often RDS resource

935
01:13:27,774 --> 01:13:31,434
description format, and it lets computers understand what data mean.

936
01:13:32,724 --> 01:13:37,134
Can you capture all of the nuances of human understanding in machine-readable

937
01:13:37,224 --> 01:13:42,654
language? Not yet. Don't know that you ever need to, but you capture quite a lot

938
01:13:42,684 --> 01:13:47,304
and enough to actually-- For again, context of use, you capture the content that

939
01:13:47,364 --> 01:13:52,944
you need for what you're trying to do and the domains where your ontology applies.

940
01:13:54,474 --> 01:13:59,334
So a couple of quick ontology examples where I've worked over the years.

941
01:13:59,785 --> 01:14:03,924
Most recently, I've been contributing to the Plant Phenology Ontology.

942
01:14:04,434 --> 01:14:10,404
It's an ontology that, again, encodes the semantics of what are the

943
01:14:10,464 --> 01:14:15,984
seasonal timings of variation of when plants put on flowers, produce fruits,

944
01:14:16,344 --> 01:14:18,414
drop their leaves, grow their new leaves in the spring.

945
01:14:18,834 --> 01:14:23,424
It's based on the Plant Ontology and the Plant Trait Ontology, which were two very

946
01:14:23,484 --> 01:14:28,344
early ontologies that got me from doing basic science into the data science world.

947
01:14:29,245 --> 01:14:34,974
And the Plant Phenology Ontology has been of great value for integrating plant

948
01:14:35,004 --> 01:14:38,904
phenology data from multiple sources, iNaturalist, National Phenology Network,

949
01:14:39,384 --> 01:14:41,814
NEON, other international phenology networks,

950
01:14:43,134 --> 01:14:46,554
into a recent study that was published doing a global analysis

951
01:14:47,904 --> 01:14:51,745
of plant phenotypes, which you can see at Rob Guralnick's talk on Thursday.

952
01:14:51,834 --> 01:14:53,124
He'll be presenting on that work.

953
01:14:54,654 --> 01:15:00,174
Another older ontology is the Biocollections Ontology, and I think actually BCO and

954
01:15:01,224 --> 01:15:05,154
a related ontology called the Population Community Ontology are the reason that I

955
01:15:05,184 --> 01:15:07,134
got involved in TADWIG to start with.

956
01:15:07,164 --> 01:15:08,214
There were some working groups

957
01:15:09,745 --> 01:15:14,424
on those two ontology back at a TADWIG meeting in Oxford many moons ago.

958
01:15:14,964 --> 01:15:20,064
So the Biology Collection Ontology or BCO is really trying to describe the

959
01:15:20,094 --> 01:15:23,334
semantics of collections and the different types of

960
01:15:25,104 --> 01:15:30,264
sampling efforts that you can have describing the relationships among the

961
01:15:30,294 --> 01:15:30,864
ontology--

962
01:15:32,544 --> 01:15:37,224
Take a break there. Describing the relationships between data that are produced and

963
01:15:37,285 --> 01:15:42,114
the sampling effort. So you might have a transect with a site within it and then a

964
01:15:42,174 --> 01:15:44,964
particular type of sampling and then repeated sampling within there.

965
01:15:45,024 --> 01:15:46,164
So those types of efforts.

966
01:15:48,745 --> 01:15:49,044
Going to

967
01:15:53,274 --> 01:15:58,344
switch gears for a second and just give a few examples from the world that I live

968
01:15:58,374 --> 01:16:01,644
in now that may be of interest to people within this community.

969
01:16:02,454 --> 01:16:07,224
A very widely used standard for real world data, that is data that's collected

970
01:16:07,344 --> 01:16:10,854
outside of clinical trials through the hospital, through

971
01:16:12,054 --> 01:16:14,184
research networks, through

972
01:16:16,254 --> 01:16:21,774
in situ studies. So data that we call real world data, which is data collected

973
01:16:21,834 --> 01:16:23,634
outside of a controlled clinical trial.

974
01:16:24,624 --> 01:16:28,914
Observational Medical Outcomes Partnerships, this is their model.

975
01:16:28,944 --> 01:16:30,745
It's a relational model.

976
01:16:32,035 --> 01:16:36,354
And as you can see, it's fairly complex, but it's really based on observations and

977
01:16:36,414 --> 01:16:41,035
measurements. So something that the BCO was really founded around.

978
01:16:41,094 --> 01:16:43,285
So it's highly relevant to the work that I do there.

979
01:16:43,584 --> 01:16:45,294
It is widely used throughout the world.

980
01:16:45,294 --> 01:16:49,854
It is what allows federated analyses of real world data across multiple sites for

981
01:16:49,884 --> 01:16:50,724
many cases.

982
01:16:53,004 --> 01:16:56,694
Again, limited semantic. This is just the data structure.

983
01:16:57,234 --> 01:17:01,495
But to go along with this, there is the OMOP vocabulary, which is--

984
01:17:03,144 --> 01:17:05,514
Many people call it an ontology. It's not.

985
01:17:05,904 --> 01:17:09,684
But it has enough structure to provide a certain level of semantics.

986
01:17:10,074 --> 01:17:14,724
And many of the terms that are used that are part of the OMOP vocabulary are pooled

987
01:17:14,785 --> 01:17:20,124
from external sources that are more ontologically structured, such as the SNOMED or

988
01:17:20,154 --> 01:17:23,304
the World Health Organization drug lists.

989
01:17:24,624 --> 01:17:30,054
And to help with the semantics, there has been implementation of the OMOP common

990
01:17:30,114 --> 01:17:35,454
data model for healthcare records coming from hospitals, which is called FHIR, Fast

991
01:17:35,514 --> 01:17:41,124
Healthcare Interoperability Resource, which is required in both the US and Europe

992
01:17:41,214 --> 01:17:44,664
if you're going to be sharing healthcare data.

993
01:17:46,584 --> 01:17:49,374
Both of these are going to become even more important as a new law is rolling

994
01:17:49,674 --> 01:17:51,354
around the European health data space.

995
01:17:52,014 --> 01:17:57,294
So might be something worth looking into as potential models for use within

996
01:17:57,354 --> 01:17:58,674
biodiversity data.

997
01:18:01,104 --> 01:18:04,194
Another quick example of some of the work that I'm doing with my team within

998
01:18:04,285 --> 01:18:06,804
Seapath is doing right now is creating a knowledge graph.

999
01:18:07,314 --> 01:18:11,394
So we have data coming in from many different sources, some very large datasets,

1000
01:18:11,814 --> 01:18:16,344
some very small. They might be structured by CDISC, SDTM standards, which is a

1001
01:18:16,404 --> 01:18:21,384
clinical trial data standard. We get a lot of non-structured data or, well, tabular

1002
01:18:21,444 --> 01:18:27,384
data from patient registries, from ultra rare diseases, where there are patient

1003
01:18:27,654 --> 01:18:30,834
groups going out and trying to collect data about the people with rare disease.

1004
01:18:31,794 --> 01:18:35,995
Get semi-structured datasets from a national organization for rare diseases, which

1005
01:18:36,024 --> 01:18:41,484
is also these, excuse me, patient organization-driven registries.

1006
01:18:41,874 --> 01:18:46,734
We have a pipeline that goes through and automatically annotates that data with

1007
01:18:46,794 --> 01:18:50,724
ontology terms, stores the annotations into our

1008
01:18:51,924 --> 01:18:56,124
internal metadata catalog called data.world, using the structure of the

1009
01:18:58,674 --> 01:19:03,864
simple standard for sharing. I always forget what SSLM stands.

1010
01:19:04,014 --> 01:19:05,274
Simple standard for sharing-

1011
01:19:07,524 --> 01:19:08,035
Instrumental?

1012
01:19:08,094 --> 01:19:12,474
I can't read that yet. Yes, but I expect many of you are familiar with it already,

1013
01:19:12,564 --> 01:19:16,434
which is great. So why is this valuable for us, this knowledge graph?

1014
01:19:17,184 --> 01:19:20,064
We are constantly expanding what we use it for.

1015
01:19:20,124 --> 01:19:24,564
But one of the key pieces has been being able to do queries across datasets.

1016
01:19:24,594 --> 01:19:28,495
Because we have a catalog where you can go as a person who signed up for it and go

1017
01:19:28,554 --> 01:19:31,254
choose datasets, do some exploration beforehand.

1018
01:19:31,254 --> 01:19:35,094
But if you want to do queries across all of the datasets, you have to have some

1019
01:19:35,124 --> 01:19:36,594
kind of common meeting across them.

1020
01:19:36,624 --> 01:19:40,794
And so this, for example, is a query looking at all the data that we have for ALS,

1021
01:19:40,854 --> 01:19:46,164
amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, searching for phenotypes and saying, "Okay, how many

1022
01:19:46,224 --> 01:19:51,174
of your datasets have data on depression or anxiety, and where can I go to get

1023
01:19:51,234 --> 01:19:51,624
those?"

1024
01:19:53,424 --> 01:19:57,624
That's something that I think is very useful when you have distributed data or data

1025
01:19:57,654 --> 01:20:01,914
that are not yet fully standardized for integration, but you want to start to ask

1026
01:20:01,974 --> 01:20:03,144
questions across them.

1027
01:20:05,364 --> 01:20:05,814
All right.

1028
01:20:07,644 --> 01:20:12,474
I started out in the beginning talking about this data to knowledge transformation.

1029
01:20:13,464 --> 01:20:17,154
You start with an observation. You collect some data about that observation.

1030
01:20:17,844 --> 01:20:22,434
You use that data and your context of use to generate evidence, and from that you

1031
01:20:22,464 --> 01:20:26,544
can derive knowledge. So data on their own are not knowledge.

1032
01:20:27,714 --> 01:20:30,684
Adding AI into the mix really raises the stakes.

1033
01:20:32,154 --> 01:20:37,614
It may help us generate new information and new knowledge, but it doesn't take away

1034
01:20:37,674 --> 01:20:43,584
the need for provenance, good data collection practices, using

1035
01:20:43,644 --> 01:20:49,554
fair data standards. So things like standards that can make data shareable, using

1036
01:20:49,644 --> 01:20:53,604
semantics that can make them interpretable, creating ontologies.

1037
01:20:55,434 --> 01:20:58,674
TADWIG helps build this infrastructure for the generation of evidence.

1038
01:20:59,184 --> 01:21:05,124
That to me is, as a scientist, the key value of TADWIG, is that you

1039
01:21:06,204 --> 01:21:12,024
take data, provide the infrastructure so that people can use it to

1040
01:21:12,084 --> 01:21:12,834
create evidence.

1041
01:21:15,594 --> 01:21:19,734
That's the number one message of what can TADWIG do?

1042
01:21:20,184 --> 01:21:26,035
Keep doing that, please. We need TADWIG in the world, so keep going.

1043
01:21:26,124 --> 01:21:30,864
It's great. The future of biodiversity science depends not just on having more

1044
01:21:30,924 --> 01:21:35,304
data, though we certainly need that, but on preserving enough meaning and context

1045
01:21:35,334 --> 01:21:36,954
for those data to become evidence.

1046
01:21:38,904 --> 01:21:39,144
So

1047
01:21:41,245 --> 01:21:44,934
take the chain at the top, turn it into what you need to do, an activity chain.

1048
01:21:45,024 --> 01:21:49,854
So you need to observe accurately. You need to preserve the meaning.

1049
01:21:50,814 --> 01:21:55,164
You need to establish trust, and only then can you generate knowledge.

1050
01:21:56,004 --> 01:21:59,424
So AI, that hasn't taken away any of those steps,

1051
01:22:00,684 --> 01:22:05,874
but it makes it even more important. It can make those steps go faster.

1052
01:22:06,804 --> 01:22:12,324
But it's more important than ever to continue to be accurate, contain meaning,

1053
01:22:13,044 --> 01:22:14,724
ensure trust, and generate knowledge.

1054
01:22:15,654 --> 01:22:20,694
So with that, I'm happy to stop and take some questions if there is time.

